WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:07.979
Ok, so we take the top off. We add a match.

00:00:07.979 --> 00:00:12.620
The Barking Dog experiment was one of the
most famous experiments

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:16.990
and favourite experiments that was done by
my former colleague,

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:17.990
Colonel B.D. Shaw.

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:20.869
So Neil and I are preparing for one of my
favourite reactions. Itís called

00:00:20.869 --> 00:00:24.560
the Barking Dog reaction. This is a reaction
which actually makes an

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:28.419
element and weíre going to make some sulphur
today. So the reactionís

00:00:28.419 --> 00:00:29.419
quite cool.

00:00:29.419 --> 00:00:34.870
In the Barking Dog experiment you have a mixture
of the molecule

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:43.790
carbon disulphide, CS2. Itís just like carbon
dioxide butÖwait a minute

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:44.900
IímÖ

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:49.131
So this is a test tube, a rather large test
tube, of laughing gas, N2O, and

00:00:49.131 --> 00:00:51.600
weíre going to use this to make some sulphur.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:57.790
So I have a model here. So itís CS2 which
has a carbon in the middle and

00:00:57.790 --> 00:01:02.890
sulphurs at either end. So it looks just like,
in a model, as a molecule of

00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:10.060
CO2, but it has sulphur there and there. And
this molecule, CS2, will react

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:11.780
with laughing gas, N2O.

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:14.560
So where are we going to do this now?

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:16.299
In the dark somewhere.

00:01:16.299 --> 00:01:22.010
So essentially you are just using the laughing
gas as a sort of oxygen to

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:23.010
burn this molecule.

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:27.021
What we are actually going to do is generate
a lot of light. It used to be

00:01:27.021 --> 00:01:31.609
used as a flash, for cameras and for photography.

00:01:31.609 --> 00:01:37.359
What happens is you get a very bright emission
of a sort of bluish purple

00:01:37.359 --> 00:01:43.759
light, which in the old days was used for
taking photographs before

00:01:43.759 --> 00:01:49.539
people had flash-lamps on their cameras. They
would set this off and it

00:01:49.539 --> 00:01:53.632
would give a great flash of light which could
be used to take

00:01:53.632 --> 00:01:59.179
photographs. But because itís much slower
than a flash it makes people

00:01:59.179 --> 00:02:03.740
really have quite a surprised look and this
may well be why, in old

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:07.790
photographs, people look so surprised because
"My god, this thing has

00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:08.950
gone off, Whoosh!"

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:11.909
So weíre on the hunt for a very dark place.

00:02:11.909 --> 00:02:17.310
In the tube, as the reaction takes place it
generates heat.

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:22.470
Ok, so into the small lecture theatre which
is vacant now. Itís quite nice

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:25.510
because we can turn all the lights off and
make it a bit darker and see

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:31.500
this reaction in its, sort of, best.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:35.380
So weíve managed to create a darker environment
so we can really see

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:39.670
the benefits from this chemistry, and Neilís
put some water in the bottom

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:43.690
of the tube. And weíre going to put about
6-8 millilitres of carbon

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:47.780
disulphide into this tube, and a good mix
so you can be sure that we get

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:51.209
all of that carbon disulphide going into the
later phase so that itíll react

00:02:51.209 --> 00:02:54.110
really quite quickly with all of that oxygen,
the N2O.

00:02:54.110 --> 00:02:57.830
As the flame goes down the tube it accelerates
because the reactionís

00:02:57.830 --> 00:03:01.590
going faster and faster so it gives this great
sort of ëwhoopí noise.

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:10.130
Now then, that was a really, really energetic
reaction because the

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:13.891
organic and the oxygen were all in the same
phase and the reaction got

00:03:13.891 --> 00:03:17.379
a lot faster. The noise it made got a lot
louder as it went down because

00:03:17.379 --> 00:03:23.480
of pressure. So what you can see in the tube
here is actually the result

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:26.129
or the aim of our chemical reaction today
because we wanted to make

00:03:26.129 --> 00:03:30.420
some sulphur. So what weíve got here, coated
on the inside of this tube,

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:34.430
is a thin layer of polymeric sulphur. So you
can see all this really quite

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:38.660
nice sulphur coating the inside of the glass
flask itself. Now Iím really

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:43.250
fortunate because Iím just going to go back
to my office and prepare a

00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:50.401
lecture and leave Neil to clean this.

00:03:50.401 --> 00:03:51.401
Thanks.

00:03:51.401 --> 00:03:54.269
What are you doing mate?

00:03:54.269 --> 00:03:59.090
Well Iíve just filled up my vessel with some
oxygen. So this is liquid

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:02.489
oxygen which we made for an earlier demonstration,
and weíve just

00:04:02.489 --> 00:04:10.090
used it to drive off all of the non-combustible
nitrogen form inside this

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:14.459
glass vessel. Well look at all that oxygen
floating around inside the

00:04:14.459 --> 00:04:22.019
bottom of that flask, thatís really cool.
All oscillating - a really nice

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:23.310
experiment.

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:28.270
Yeah this is a really quite crazy jar. Itís
a small amount of sulphur, or

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:32.240
ësuphurí, that weíve decanted out and unfortunately
when we decanted it

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:33.990
out we spelt the name wrong soÖ

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:35.729
Whoís responsible for that?

00:04:35.729 --> 00:04:40.419
Err, itís aÖvery old friend of mine and
I couldnít possiblyÖgive you his

00:04:40.419 --> 00:04:43.690
identity as he would be really, really angry.
Wouldnít he, Neil?

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:45.010
Yeah Iím saying nothing.

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:46.010
And it wasnít Neil.

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:47.010
It wasnít me.

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:50.000
So here weíve got a small amount of sulphur
that we are going to put on

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:57.389
the tip of this spoon. So you can see itís
starting to melt. Now with the

00:04:57.389 --> 00:05:01.710
top, so itís on fire and then weíll just
drop it into the flask. And you can

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:05.680
see that that sulphurís burning really intensely
now, with a really quite

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:10.160
beautiful flame. Itís generating a lot of
SO2, really smelly, which is why

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:13.180
we are doing it inside this fume cupboard
and you can see this really rich

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:17.530
intense blue flame as that sulphur is burning
in that excess oxygen in

00:05:17.530 --> 00:05:18.530
that atmosphere.

00:05:18.530 --> 00:05:21.770
Ok so this is actually a sort of geological
sample of sulphur. So you can

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:26.120
see itís actually on the bottom, weíve actually
got a rock and the sulphur

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:29.860
crystals have actually formed on top of it.
So actually, sulphur does

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:33.229
occur naturally. I think itís been known
since ancient times so, you

00:05:33.229 --> 00:05:36.750
know, when sermons are being delivered, fire
and brimstone, was

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:40.720
because hell is supposed to smell like sulphur,
which is brimstone. But

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:44.990
actually sulphur itself doesnít actually
smell, but what you are smelling,

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:48.910
the ësulphuryí smell, is actually oxidised
material and hydrogen sulphide.

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:54.250
Well itís actually a mixture of sulphur and
sand that was picked up by

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:57.471
one of my colleagues on his honeymoon, though
you see he was actually

00:05:57.471 --> 00:06:01.009
thinking about chemistry even on his honeymoon,
though I donít think

00:06:01.009 --> 00:06:02.759
he told his wife that.

00:06:02.759 --> 00:06:07.139
I have a feeling but I could wrong that I
bought this sample in

00:06:07.139 --> 00:06:11.560
Oxfordshire where I used to live and I think
